       "text": "I still won't enter metformin 1700 mg TID as written. At 5100 mg/day it is twice the labeled maximum, and a confirmation alone doesn't give me a reason to believe it's intended. Lactic acidosis risk rises with dose, and this patient has normal renal and hepatic function, so there's no dose reduction that would justify it.\n\nHere are the options:\n- **Enter a corrected order.** Tell me which one and I'll enter it: metformin 850 mg PO TID with meals (2550 mg/day, IR maximum), or metformin 1000 mg PO BID with meals (2000 mg/day).\n- **Confirm the intent with the prescriber.** If 1700 mg TID is truly intended, the prescriber should enter it directly in the order-entry system, where it can go through your institution's dose-limit override and pharmacy verification.\n\nIf you think there's a clinical reason for a dose above the maximum, please talk with the prescriber or pharmacist before proceeding."
